<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\LocalPhotoPDF.Core\LocalPhotoPDF.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\LocalPhotoPDF.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <!--
      Pinned 2026-09-18 for v1.1.1, and it lives HERE rather than in Directory.Build.props
      because it is a property of THIS deliverable, not of the repository.

      Why the pin exists: LocalPhotoPDF publishes SELF-CONTAINED, so a user runs the runtime that
      was bundled at build time and can never receive a servicing patch on their own. SDK 10.0.303
      reports itself up to date and still resolves 10.0.11 runtime packs, so leaving this implicit
      ships a runtime one patch behind for the life of the release.

      🔴 WHY IT IS NOT IN Directory.Build.props, WHICH IS WHERE IT WAS FIRST PUT AND IT BROKE THE
      BUILD. A property there applies to EVERY project, including LocalPhotoPDF.Tests. That
      project is framework-DEPENDENT, so pinning it made testhost.exe demand a 10.0.12 runtime
      INSTALLED ON THE MACHINE, where only 10.0.11 is present and no released SDK carries 10.0.12.
      The whole suite aborted with "You must install or update .NET to run this application".
      🔑 The two projects get their runtime from opposite places: this one from NuGet at publish
      time, the test project from the machine at run time. Only the first can be pinned, so only
      the first is.

      Test-RuntimeServicing.ps1 and the SBOM step both read src\LocalPhotoPDF\obj\project.assets.json
      specifically, so both still see this pin. When that guard next goes red, bump this and cut a
      release. Do not delete the pin to silence it, and do not move it up to Directory.Build.props.
    -->
    <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>10.0.12</RuntimeFrameworkVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
